# Gitflow for Linear tickets

Use Gitflow. One Linear ticket = one branch. Do not commit ticket work to `main` or `develop`.

## Long-lived branches

- `main` — production / released history
- `develop` — integration. Create it from `main` if it does not exist yet.

## Per-ticket branches

Start every ticket from an up-to-date `develop`:

```bash
git checkout develop
git pull
git checkout -b feature/jib-486-add-simulated-time
```

Name: `feature/<ticket-id>-<short-kebab-description>` (lowercase).

```text
✅ feature/jib-486-add-simulated-time
❌ cursor/world-and-simtime
❌ main
❌ feature/simtime-and-tick   # two tickets
```

- Features and ticket bugs: `feature/…` from `develop`, PR back to `develop`.
- Production emergency: `hotfix/jib-xxx-…` from `main`, merge to `main` and `develop`.
- Milestone ship: `release/m0-empty-universe` from `develop`, merge to `main` and `develop`.

## Agent workflow

1. When a ticket is opened, create/check out its `feature/` branch before editing code.
2. Keep the branch to that ticket only. Next ticket = new branch from `develop`.
3. After the ticket is done: commit on the feature branch, PR into `develop`. Do not merge to `main` unless this is a release or hotfix.
4. Do not use `cursor/` as the ticket branch prefix.
